"Shows the locations and relationships of selected physical and human features so that readers can orient themselves geographically.\n\nUse when: Explain where a place sits relative to recognizable surroundings. Provide a restrained location context for a project or service.\n\nAvoid when: The intended message is a thematic rate comparison, legal boundary determination or precise navigation unsupported by the source.\n\nVisual grammar: Point, line and area symbols distinguish feature classes. Labels identify relevant places at the selected scale. Projection, scale and orientation provide the spatial reference needed for interpretation.\n\nBuild: Define the audience, geographic extent and location question. Select reliable features and a suitable projection and display scale. Generalize nonessential detail while retaining meaningful spatial relationships.\n\nCheck: Feature locations agree with the source and coordinate system. Labels and symbol priorities match the intended scale. Geographic intersections remain truthful; simplify through layers or separate views.\n\nPitfalls: Adding dense background detail that hides the location question. Presenting schematic distances as if they were measured geography.\n\nExample: A visitor guide locates a nature center relative to nearby towns, main roads and the river, using a small number of clear labels.\n\nAlternatives: Topographic map, Transit map."
